All working. The terminal animation and the HTML are both live in `/workspace`.

I built a small, self-contained thing: a **cellular-automata playground** in a single pure-Python file (`life.py`), no dependencies.

**What you can do with it:**
- `python3 life.py glider life` — watch a glider glide across the terminal
- `python3 life.py r-pentomino life -g 200` — the famous chaotic seed explode and settle
- `python3 life.py soup maze` — random soup under any of the 7 rule sets (Life, HighLife, Seeds, DayNight, Maze, Pulsar, Fredkin)
- `python3 life.py --html gosper -o life.html` — exports a self-contained animated page you can open in a browser (click to pause/resume)

**What I verified before wrapping up:**
- The stable block stays stable; the blinker oscillates at exactly period 2 — the Life invariants hold
- The Gosper gun sustains life over 60 generations and doesn't decay
- All 7 rule sets run cleanly, terminal and HTML both work, and `life.html` is well-formed with a valid embedded JSON frame payload
- `py_compile` passes

I also fixed a real bug along the way: the step function was coupled to module-wide grid constants, so it crashed on any grid of a different size — it now derives its bounds from the grid itself. If you open `life.html`, you'll see the Gosper gun spraying gliders in a dark, GitHub-style theme, and you can click the canvas to toggle the animation.

